WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.294 --> 00:00:07.785
It was seven o'clock the next evening, but Bill Sikes was just waking from a nap. He had been gravely ill for weeks.

2
00:00:07.786 --> 00:00:14.960
"How do you feel tonight, Bill?" Nancy sat sewing in a corner while the white dog sat loyally next to Sikes' bed.

3
00:00:14.961 --> 00:00:20.769
"Weak, very weak. Hurry up now. Help me out of bed."

4
00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:28.610
Nancy had just settled Bill into a chair when there was a knock at the door. In walked Fagin, Charley Bates, and the Dodger.

5
00:00:28.611 --> 00:00:32.428
"What evil wind blew you here, Fagin?" asked Bill.

6
00:00:32.429 --> 00:00:40.190
"No evil wind at all, my dear. Dodger, open the bundle and give Bill the trifles we spent all our money on."

7
00:00:40.191 --> 00:00:46.936
The Dodger opened the bundle to reveal a rabbit pie, some strong tea, some sugar, and a bottle of wine.

8
00:00:46.937 --> 00:00:52.595
Sikes did not seem impressed. "I need some money tonight, Fagin."

9
00:00:52.596 --> 00:00:56.325
"I don't have a single coin with me," Fagin replied.

10
00:00:56.326 --> 00:00:58.333
"You have lots at home."

11
00:00:58.334 --> 00:01:01.246
"Well, I could send the Dodger home to get—"

12
00:01:01.247 --> 00:01:08.000
"No, no," Sikes interrupted. "He'd forget to come back. Nancy will go with you."

13
00:01:08.001 --> 00:01:12.921
So Nancy soon found herself at Fagin's, waiting for him to count out some coins.

14
00:01:12.922 --> 00:01:20.984
Suddenly they heard footsteps in the hall. Just as Fagin went to the door, it was opened by a man in a dark cloak. Monks!

15
00:01:20.985 --> 00:01:24.325
"Who's this?" Monks pointed at Nancy.

16
00:01:24.326 --> 00:01:27.730
"Just one of my young people," said Fagin.

17
00:01:27.731 --> 00:01:33.285
Monks stared at her and then said to Fagin, "I need to speak to you in private."

18
00:01:33.286 --> 00:01:40.722
Fagin led him to a room upstairs. Nancy waited a few moments and then she quietly followed them.

19
00:01:40.723 --> 00:01:49.381
She stood outside the room, eavesdropping on their conversation. When she heard them push back their chairs, she ran softly downstairs.

20
00:01:49.382 --> 00:01:55.611
"Are you all right?" Fagin asked her after Monks had left. "You look quite pale."

21
00:01:55.612 --> 00:02:03.690
Nancy looked at the floor and didn't answer his question. "Bill will be angry if I don't return soon. Do you have the money?"

22
00:02:03.691 --> 00:02:07.769
Fagin handed her some coins, and she went back to Bill Sikes.

23
00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:13.690
The next night Nancy added something to Sikes' drink to make him sleep so she could go out for a few hours.

24
00:02:13.691 --> 00:02:18.174
"It will take me at least an hour to walk there," she muttered as she set out.

25
00:02:18.175 --> 00:02:27.682
When she reached the wealthy part of London, the clock was striking eleven. At last she came to her destination: a small hotel on a quiet street.

26
00:02:27.683 --> 00:02:34.333
Nancy knew she didn't belong in this posh area, but she squared her shoulders and pushed open the door.

27
00:02:34.334 --> 00:02:38.507
"Who do you want?" said the porter. He gave her a critical look.

28
00:02:38.508 --> 00:02:42.595
"A lady staying here. Miss Rose Maylie."

29
00:02:42.596 --> 00:02:44.928
"And who shall I say is calling?"

30
00:02:44.929 --> 00:02:49.904
"My name won't mean anything to her. I must speak to her myself."

31
00:02:49.905 --> 00:02:53.904
The porter grumbled, but he found a boy to take a message to Rose.

32
00:02:53.905 --> 00:03:01.325
Meanwhile he led Nancy into a room to wait. At last the door opened and a beautiful young woman entered the room.

33
00:03:01.326 --> 00:03:05.991
"I am Rose Maylie," said the young woman, looking at Nancy's ragged clothes.

34
00:03:05.992 --> 00:03:13.880
"What can I do for you? If you're in poverty or in trouble, I'll try my best to help you. Please sit down."

35
00:03:13.881 --> 00:03:22.229
"Let me stand. I'm about to put my life and the lives of others in your hands." Nancy began to weep.

36
00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:26.785
"I'm the one who dragged Oliver back to Fagin when he was on his way to the bookseller's."

37
00:03:26.786 --> 00:03:29.539
"You!" Rose was shocked.

38
00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:37.285
"I've sneaked away from people who would surely murder me if they knew I'd come here to tell you something. Do you know a man named Monks?"

39
00:03:37.286 --> 00:03:39.833
"No," said Rose.

40
00:03:39.834 --> 00:03:45.278
"He knows you, and he knew you were staying here. That's how I found you."

41
00:03:45.278 --> 00:03:49.031
Rose was alarmed. "But who is he?"

42
00:03:49.032 --> 00:03:56.872
"Someone who knows Fagin. Soon after Oliver arrived at your house, I overheard a conversation between Monks and Fagin.

43
00:03:56.873 --> 00:04:03.864
Monks accidentally saw Oliver on the day we first lost him, the day Oliver was accused of stealing a gentleman's handkerchief."

44
00:04:03.865 --> 00:04:11.841
"Oh yes." Rose nodded. "I remember Oliver telling us that was the day he went to live with Mr. Brownlow."

45
00:04:11.842 --> 00:04:19.087
"Monks had been looking for Oliver, but I don't know why," Nancy went on. "So Monks made a deal with Fagin.

46
00:04:19.088 --> 00:04:23.666
Get Oliver back from Mr. Brownlow, and Monks would give Fagin some money.

47
00:04:23.667 --> 00:04:28.492
If Fagin could turn Oliver into a criminal, Fagin would receive even more money."

48
00:04:28.493 --> 00:04:33.738
Rose was puzzled. "Why would Monks want Oliver turned into a criminal?"

49
00:04:33.739 --> 00:04:39.913
"I don't know. Monks caught sight of my shadow on the wall that night, and I had to flee the house."

50
00:04:39.914 --> 00:04:42.325
"Did you ever see Monks again?"

51
00:04:42.326 --> 00:04:48.825
"Not until last night when he came to Fagin's. I eavesdropped again when they went upstairs to talk.

52
00:04:48.826 --> 00:04:53.468
Monks boasted that he'd tossed in the river the only proof of Oliver's identity.

53
00:04:53.469 --> 00:04:59.889
He said that Oliver was his younger brother, and he'd now secured all of their father's inheritance for himself."

54
00:04:59.890 --> 00:05:03.055
"His brother!" exclaimed Rose.

55
00:05:03.056 --> 00:05:10.460
"Those were his words." Nancy glanced uneasily around, fearful that somehow Sikes had managed to follow her.

56
00:05:10.461 --> 00:05:17.373
"I must leave now. I have to reach home before anyone suspects that I've been out on an errand such as this."

57
00:05:17.374 --> 00:05:28.548
"No, wait," said Rose. "I can summon a gentleman who will help you. If you repeat this information to him, he'll see that you are sent immediately to a place of safety."

58
00:05:28.549 --> 00:05:39.675
"I want to go home. How can I explain this to you? Among the men that I've described, there is one I can't leave. He's the worst of all, but I love him."

59
00:05:39.676 --> 00:05:50.026
"Oh, don't go back to him!" Tears ran down Rose's face. "You've risked your life for Oliver. Please, let me save you."

60
00:05:50.027 --> 00:06:00.764
Nancy sank to her knees. "Dear, sweet angel. You're the first person who has been kind to me. But it's too late to save me from a life of crime."

61
00:06:00.765 --> 00:06:02.882
"It's never too late."

62
00:06:02.883 --> 00:06:09.374
"It is. If I told anyone else what I've told you, the man I love would be sure to hang. "

63
00:06:09.375 --> 00:06:12.253
Nancy rose and turned toward the door.

64
00:06:12.253 --> 00:06:17.831
"Wait!" said Rose. "Where can I find you if I need to speak to you again?"

65
00:06:17.832 --> 00:06:25.156
"I'll wait for you on London Bridge every Sunday night between eleven o'clock and midnight. That is, if I'm still alive."

66
00:06:25.157 --> 00:06:27.569
"At least take some money from me."

67
00:06:27.570 --> 00:06:30.445
"Not a penny," replied Nancy.

68
00:06:30.445 --> 00:06:38.667
And with a sob, she left the room. Rose sank into a chair, wondering what to do next.

